The user-mode test `test_get_state` is causing a segmentation fault. This test is part of the `can_classic` test suite, which contains a mix of supervisor-mode (`ZTEST`) and user-mode (`ZTEST_USER`) tests.

According to the Zephyr documentation, for mixed test suites, the setup function runs in a supervisor context. Any kernel objects required by user-mode tests must have their permissions explicitly granted to the user-mode test thread.

The `can_classic_setup` function calls `can_common_test_setup`. In `can_common_test_setup`, permissions for `can_dev` and `can_msgq` are granted to `k_current_get()`. In a mixed suite, `k_current_get()` refers to the supervisor thread, not the user-mode thread where `test_get_state` executes. This lack of permissions for the user-mode thread is the root cause of the segmentation fault.

The fix is to modify `can_common_test_setup` to grant permissions to the user-mode thread when `CONFIG_TEST_USERSPACE` is enabled and a user thread exists for the test suite. For builds without `CONFIG_TEST_USERSPACE`, the original behavior of granting permissions to the current thread is preserved.

